  "id": "Kingston-26_00004_FUL",
  "appeal_status": null,
  "appeal_start_date": null,
  "description": "Change of use of retail unit to C1 serviced accommodation",
  "development_type": "Other change of use",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The development hereby permitted shall begin before the expiration of three years from the date of this permission.\r\rReason: This is required by Section 91 of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 (as amended).",
    "The development shall be carried out in accordance with the approved Fire Statement and thereafter maintained. \r\rReason: In the interests of fire safety and to ensure the safety of all building users, in accordance with Policy D12 of the London Plan 2021.",
    "The development hereby approved shall be designed and constructed to achieve a maximum water consumption of no more than 105 litres per person per day (as calculated in accordance with the Building Regulations Optional Requirement G2), and shall thereafter be retained as such.\r\rReason: To ensure the development is sustainable and minimises water consumption in accordance with Policy SI5 of the London Plan (2021) and Policy DM3 of the LDF Core Strategy (2012).",
    "The development hereby permitted shall be used for C1 Serviced Accommodation only and for no other purpose, including any other purpose within Class C of the Schedule to the Town and Country Planning (Use Classes) Order 1987 (as amended). The units shall be used for short-term holiday/business letting only and shall not be occupied by any person as their sole or principal place of residence. The owner/operator shall maintain a register of all occupiers, including their names and their main home addresses, and shall make this register available for inspection by the Local Planning Authority at all reasonable times.\r\rReason: To ensure the development is not used for permanent residential occupation (Use Class C3), for which it is unsuitable due to physical constraints regarding outlook and the lack of private amenity space. Permanent residency would result in substandard living conditions for occupiers and potential conflict with the adjacent public house, contrary to Policies D6 and D13 of the London Plan (2021) and Policies DM10 and DM13 of the Core Strategy (2012)"
  ],
